READING IT.

The term did what it was built to do.  More checks, deeper material investments held with a
non-negative score, and a peak attack index a full step above the baseline's -- it seeks the
pressure the baseline only stumbles into.  It also never paid: not one of the six conviction
games was won.

But six games of 301 cannot explain a 12-point win-rate gap.  The damage is diffuse, not a
handful of spectacular sacrifices.  That matters for attribution: the failure king-safety.md 4.4
was built to prevent -- steering into a sacrifice whose compensation the material-only shortcut
then hides, the combination that cost the Audax fork 67 Elo -- is NOT what happened here.  The
cap held.  What is left is the failure mode roadmap 12.21 named after the first attempt and has
now measured four times: static noise added to a search that already resolves king attacks
tactically.  Its own mean score moved from +10 to -5 cp, so it rates its own positions slightly
worse and plays them a great deal worse.

WHAT THIS DOES NOT SETTLE.

Three BlunderTest cases were recorded BEFORE the match as boundaries the term provably does not
reach, and a result of -42.9 touches none of them:

  castling960_atMove5           zero attack units at the moment of the decision
  staticEval960_afterDxc6       term contributes 20 cp against a 475 cp gap
  qxd7_vsStudylovers            term ranks the blunder first of 36, pointing the wrong way

And the yardstick.  This was standard chess; roadmap-backlog.md calls Chess960 the decisive
measurement for this parameter class, on the argument that a castled king on g1 gives a
king-safety term almost nothing to measure.  The rule stated in advance was that a NEUTRAL
standard result would be inconclusive.  This one is not neutral, so by that rule it settles the
question for standard chess without a second run.  Whether it settles 960 is a separate question;
the curve, the tooling and the prepared 960 invocation all survive to answer it.

Do not quote -42.9 as "king safety does not work".
